<Layout
	title="About Me"
	description="DevOps engineer, CKA certified, based in Croatia."
	pubDate={new Date('Jun 03 2026')}
>
	<p>
		I'm Filip Corluka, a DevOps engineer based in Croatia, EU. I've spent the better part of a
		decade working on cloud-native infrastructure — mostly at the intersection of automation,
		security, and reliability.
	</p>

	<p>
		Day-to-day I work on Kubernetes cluster administration, CI/CD pipeline design, container
		security, infrastructure-as-code (Terraform, Ansible), GitOps with ArgoCD, and observability.
		I hold a <strong>Certified Kubernetes Administrator (CKA)</strong> certification and work
		across AWS, Azure, GCP, and Hetzner environments.
	</p>

	<p>
		Beyond client work, I'm founder of <a href="https://progrex.dev" target="_blank">Progrex</a>,
		where I focus on turning manual security audit workflows into automated pipelines.
	</p>

	<p>
		This blog is where I write about things I find useful or worth documenting in the DevOps space.
		Posts tend to be practical and reproducible — if there's a CLI command, you can run it yourself.
	</p>

	<h2>Setup</h2>
	<p>
		I work on macOS. My terminal of choice is <strong>iTerm2</strong> with a custom color scheme I
		built myself. For code I use <strong>VS Code</strong>, and I run <strong>zsh</strong> as my
		shell.
	</p>

	<h2>Outside of work</h2>
	<p>
		My biggest hobby outside of tech is darts. I play whenever I get the chance — it's one of those
		games that looks simple but rewards a lot of patience and consistency, which I appreciate. There's
		something satisfying about the focus it demands; no notifications, no context switching, just you
		and the board.
	</p>
	<p>
		My current setup is a <strong>Harrows NX 90% Tungsten</strong> in a bomber style barrel, paired
		with <strong>K-Flex No.2 intermediate flights</strong> and <strong>35mm Harrows shafts</strong>.
		Still dialing it in, but it feels right. For the board I'm rocking a <strong>Target TOR</strong>
		— super satisfied with how it performs.
	</p>
	<p>
		Earlier this year I hit my first ever <strong>180</strong> . Pic or it didn't happen.
	</p>
